Slide 2\
The Problem:\
In India, around 50% of visually disabled people are uneducated, and only 5% receive any educational assistance. This leaves 35 million people in India without access to education, including subjects like science and math. Globally, 90 million children need access to education but don't have it.\
\
Slide 3 and 4\
UNICEF and SDGs:\
According to UNICEF, India's and the world's current Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) is to provide education to visually impaired people, with 1 out of every 10 blind individuals lacking access.\
\
Slide 6\
Introducing eDrishti: \
To address this critical issue, we formed eDrishti. eDrishti is not just a company; it's a vision to provide accessible STEM education to 45% of visually impaired Indians. We offer software connected with hardware via Bluetooth, enabling blind people to learn science and maths.\
\
Slide 7\
Our Product:\
We have a vast curriculum curated by blind tutors, live figure tracing functionality, and voice-navigated mobile app. The pain point we address is that Braille, the language for study, does not support higher education curriculum for science and maths, leading to illiteracy and less employment among blind people. Our solution provides accessible content and easy-to-interpret graphical data through hardware to help them excel in their careers.\
\
Slide 8\
Feedback and Support: \
After developing the prototype, we gathered feedback. In a recent discussion, Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella, supporting our cause, firmly believes that eDrishti would revolutionise blind education.\
\
